= Mulungu

_Status: a Brazilian native_ Fabaceae _tree of the_ Erythrina _genus — principally_ Erythrina mulungu _Benth. (Brazilian Cerrado and Atlantic Forest) and_ Erythrina velutina _Willd. (Northeast Brazilian Caatinga, also called_ suinã); _the_ bark _is the principal drug-form. Foundational to Brazilian folk medicine as the canonical_ anxiolytic-and-sedative _native botanical — the substance most consistently cited in Brazilian ethnobotanical surveys for anxiety, insomnia, and nervous-pattern disorders, and one of the few Brazilian native medicinals with substantive modern psychopharmacological research support. The vernacular name "mulungu" is a_ Bantu (Kimbundu / Kikongo) loanword _brought by Africans transported to Brazil through the Atlantic slave trade — the term meaning broadly "tree" or "sacred tree / god" in the source languages — and the substance carries a continuous_ African-Brazilian / Afro-Brazilian cultural-and-medical heritage _through the Candomblé and Umbanda religious-medical traditions alongside the indigenous Tupi and caboclo registers. Not classical in any of the four canonical Eurasian medical traditions; exclusively American with substantial African-Brazilian cultural-and-religious overlay._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Erythrina mulungu_ Benth., family Fabaceae (subfamily Faboideae / Papilionoideae), tribe Phaseoleae. Older synonyms include _Erythrina verna_ Vell. and _Erythrina aurantiaca_ Ridl. — the species nomenclatural history has been somewhat unstable; the modern accepted name is _E. mulungu_. The drug is the *bark* (cortex; trunk-bark, sometimes also root-bark) — the leaves and flowers are less commonly used. The closely-related Brazilian / Latin-American _Erythrina_ species variously used as mulungu:
- *_Erythrina velutina_ Willd.* — Northeast Brazilian Caatinga; also called *suinã*; the principal mulungu species of the Brazilian Northeast and the most-studied for psychopharmacology after _E. mulungu_.
- *_Erythrina speciosa_ Andrews* — São Paulo and Southern Brazilian; also called *eritrina-candelabro*; ornamental and medicinal.
- *_Erythrina crista-galli_ L.* — *ceibo* (Argentine national flower / national tree of Uruguay) — distinct species, also called mulungu in some Brazilian Southern-region usages; substantial Argentine-Uruguayan folk-medical tradition.
- *_Erythrina poeppigiana_ (Walp.) O.F.Cook* — Andean / Amazonian species.

*The genus_ Erythrina_ contains ~130 species pantropically distributed, all characterized by the production of _Erythrina alkaloids_ — a distinctive class of tetracyclic alkaloids with neuromuscular and CNS pharmacology. The South Asian _Erythrina variegata_ has classical Ayurvedic use (_pāribhadra_ / _mandāra_) as a distinct substance.*

_Erythrina_ from Greek ἐρυθρός (_erythrós_, "red") — for the bright-red flowers (the canonical "coral tree" of English usage); _mulungu_ as species epithet preserves the Bantu vernacular in the Linnaean binomial — one of the cleaner cases of an African-Brazilian loanword reaching the international botanical nomenclature.#footnote[Species identity and synonymy: _Flora do Brasil_ (Jardim Botânico do Rio de Janeiro); standard South-American Fabaceae botanical literature; the species nomenclatural complexity is acknowledged in current ethnobotanical literature. The genus _Erythrina_ pantropical distribution and the alkaloid-class chemistry are well documented in standard pharmacognosy.]

*English:* *Coral tree* (the broader genus name in English ornamental horticulture); *Brazilian coral tree*, *mulungu* (the Portuguese-derived term used in English specialty herbal-medicine literature).

*Portuguese (Brazilian):* *mulungu* — the canonical name; from *Bantu* (Kimbundu and Kikongo languages of West-Central Africa). The Kimbundu *mulúngu* / *mulungu* meant variously *"god / sky / sacred tree"* in different contexts; the term was *brought to Brazil by enslaved Bantu-speaking Africans* (principally from Angola, Congo, and the broader Lower-Guinea coast) during the Atlantic slave trade (16th-19th c.), and was *applied to the native Brazilian Erythrina species* — a substance that the Bantu Africans recognized as functionally analogous to the medicinal-and-sacred Erythrina species of West Africa. The vernacular preserves an unbroken African-Brazilian cultural-and-medical transmission. Regional variants and related vernaculars:
- *Mulungu* (general Brazilian) — _E. mulungu_, sometimes _E. velutina_.
- *Mulungu-coral* (Atlantic Forest region) — the red-flowered _E. crista-galli_.
- *Suinã* (Northeast Brazilian, Tupi-derived) — _E. velutina_, the principal Northeastern mulungu species.
- *Corticeira* — older Portuguese-Brazilian vernacular for the trunk-bark.
- *Bico-de-papagaio* ("parrot's beak") — the flower-shape register.
- *Sapatinho-de-judeu* ("Jew's little shoe") — another flower-shape vernacular.
- *Eritrina-mulungu*, *mulungu-do-litoral* — modern technical vernaculars.

*Spanish (Argentine-Uruguayan, for _E. crista-galli_):* *ceibo* — the national flower of Argentina (since 1942) and the national tree of Uruguay; substantial Río-de-la-Plata folk-medical and cultural register.

*Tupi-Guarani:* *suinã* (Tupi, for _E. velutina_); *kuruchi* / *kurukantsi* (various regional Tupi names for Erythrina species).

*African-Bantu (Kimbundu, Kikongo):* *mulúngu* (Kimbundu) — variously "tree / sky / god"; *mulungu* (Kikongo) — similar polysemic register. *The substance's name is essentially the only African-Brazilian loanword reaching the international plant-nomenclature register — a marker of the depth of African cultural-medical transmission to Brazilian-folk pharmacy.* Other Bantu-Brazilian medical-and-cultural loanwords (e.g. _quitanda_, _moleque_, _samba_, _candomblé_) operate in non-medical cultural registers; *mulungu* is the exceptional case of a substance-name persisting into Linnaean botanical nomenclature.#footnote[Bantu etymology: standard African-Brazilian linguistic and cultural-history literature; the *mulungu* / *mulúngu* Bantu polysemy is documented in standard Kimbundu and Kikongo lexicography. The Atlantic slave trade transmission of the term and the substance-naming continuity is well-documented in Brazilian Afro-religion and ethnobotanical literature; cf. Reginaldo Prandi's work on Afro-Brazilian religion and the broader Brazilian-cultural literature on Bantu inheritance.]

*Sanskrit / Hindi:* No species-specific entry for the Brazilian Erythrina. The South-Asian _Erythrina variegata_ L. (= _E. indica_ Lam.) is canonical in Ayurveda under पारिभद्र (*pāribhadra*) and मन्दार (*mandāra*); these are distinct species with parallel but distinct medical use (principally for vermicidal and joint-pain registers rather than the anxiolytic register central to Brazilian _E. mulungu_).

*Chinese:* No canonical Chinese entry for the Brazilian species. The genus _Erythrina_ has Asian species (notably _E. variegata_ in Taiwan and Southern China) with vernacular Chinese names but minimal classical pharmacology.

*Arabic / Persian:* No canonical Tibb entry — exclusively American species in the South-American sense.

== Classical Brazilian / Indigenous / Afro-Brazilian tradition

_This is the substantive tradition for mulungu — and is unusually rich among Brazilian native medicinals for the_ tri-stream cultural-and-medical continuity _of (a) indigenous Tupi-and-Guarani, (b) caboclo-creole, and (c) Afro-Brazilian (Bantu-derived, mediated through Candomblé and Umbanda)._

*Indigenous Tupi-Guarani:* mulungu / suinã / kuruchi bark used as a *calmante* (sedative-anxiolytic) infusion; topical preparations for wounds and inflammation; the bright-red flowers in some traditions used as *ritual offerings* for healing ceremonies.

*Caboclo (rural creole) Brazilian tradition:* the *substantial mainstream Brazilian folk-medical register* — mulungu as the canonical *Brazilian native anxiolytic-and-sleep-promoting bark* — taken as:
- *Chá de mulungu* (mulungu tea) — short decoction of the bark, ~3-5 g in 250 mL; the canonical Brazilian folk preparation for anxiety, insomnia, palpitations, nervous restlessness, and emotional disturbance. Taken in the evening.
- *Tintura de mulungu* (mulungu tincture) — alcoholic maceration; concentrated form.
- *Xarope de mulungu* — combined with other Brazilian sedative-cardiac herbs (passion fruit leaves, lemongrass).
- *Garrafada de mulungu* — Brazilian compound folk-medical decoction in a glass bottle, often with multiple co-herbs.

*Afro-Brazilian (Candomblé and Umbanda) tradition:* mulungu in *banhos* (ritual baths) for spiritual calming, protection, and removal of *energias negativas* (negative energies); the substance associated with certain *orixás* (Yoruba-Bantu deities preserved in Afro-Brazilian religion) — notably *Ossain* (the orixá of medicinal plants and herbal knowledge in the Yoruba register) and certain Bantu-derived *nkisi* deities. The *cleansing-and-spiritual-protection* register parallels the Sanskrit *bhūtaghna* (apotropaic) attribution of garlic (see _garlic.typ_) and reflects the cross-cultural recognition that pharmacologically-potent CNS-active substances carry both medicinal and spiritual significance.

*Brazilian Pharmacopoeia and modern phytotherapy:* mulungu was registered in the Anvisa-approved *RENISUS* (Relação Nacional de Plantas Medicinais de Interesse ao SUS, the National Relation of Medicinal Plants of Interest to the Brazilian Public Health System) and is one of the most-studied Brazilian native medicinals for psychiatric-folk applications.#footnote[Brazilian ethnobotanical and Afro-Brazilian religious literature: Lorenzi & Matos, _Plantas Medicinais no Brasil_; Reginaldo Prandi, _Mitologia dos Orixás_; standard Candomblé and Umbanda religious-medical literature. Brazilian Pharmacopoeia (Farmacopeia Brasileira); Anvisa RENISUS. Modern psychopharmacological research on _Erythrina mulungu_ and _E. velutina_: published Brazilian research in _Journal of Ethnopharmacology_, _Phytomedicine_, _Pharmacology Biochemistry and Behavior_.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_Not classical for the Brazilian species. Reasoned cross-system placement only._

By reasoned placement on the anxiolytic-and-sedative axis (anchored on the Brazilian folk register and the modern erythrina-alkaloid pharmacology, with closest parallel to the canonical Chinese *安神 anshen* class of spirit-calming substances such as 酸棗仁 _suānzǎorén_ (jujube seed) and 遠志 _yuǎn zhì_ (Polygala)): neutral to slightly cool (平/微涼); bitter (苦); Heart (心), Liver (肝). Reasoned actions: 安神鎮靜 (calms the spirit and tranquilizes), 養心 (nourishes the Heart), 平肝 (calms the Liver), 解鬱 (resolves emotional constraint), 通絡 (opens the collaterals for the anti-inflammatory action). Cautions: hypotensive register; cardiac patients; pregnancy. Reasoned, not citation.

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Not classical for the Brazilian species. The related_ Erythrina variegata _is canonical in Ayurveda under_ पारिभद्र _(pāribhadra) and_ मन्दार _(mandāra) but is a distinct species with distinct pharmacology (more vermicidal-and-joint-pain register, less anxiolytic register). The Brazilian_ E. mulungu _has no canonical Sanskrit entry. Reasoned cross-system placement only._

*Reasoned rasa:* Tikta (bitter — strongly), Kaṣāya (astringent — secondary, the bark-tannin fraction).

*Reasoned guṇa:* Guru (heavy — modest, the sedative-grounding register), Snigdha (unctuous — modest), Manda (slow — distinctive for a CNS-depressant substance), Sthira (stable — the sustained sedative action).

*Reasoned vīrya:* Śīta (cooling) — modest.

*Reasoned vipāka:* Kaṭu (pungent).

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Pacifies *Vāta* (strongly — the manda-sthira-guru-snigdha-śīta profile is the canonical Vāta-pacifying axis; Vāta-pattern anxiety, insomnia, nervous tremor, palpitation are the principal indications); pacifies Pitta in modest dose (the cooling-bitter register); mildly Kapha-aggravating in heavy / chronic use.

*Reasoned prabhāva:* the *specific anxiolytic-sedative-anticonvulsant* combined action, mediated mechanistically by the *Erythrina alkaloid binding to GABA-A and nicotinic-cholinergic receptors*, gives mulungu a prabhāva-level register paralleling the classical Ayurvedic _medhya-rasāyana_ class and the Chinese 安神 _anshen_ class. The substance addresses what the Ayurvedic tradition would frame as *vāta-pradhāna manasika-vyādhi* (Vāta-predominant mental disorders) — anxiety, insomnia, restlessness, nervous tremor — with mechanistic-and-clinical efficacy. Reasoned, not citation.

*Reasoned karma:* _Manas-prasādana_ (mind-calming — the canonical _bhāvanā_ for sedative-anxiolytic drugs); _nidrā-janana_ (sleep-promoting); _hṛd-rakṣaka_ (cardiotonic, modest); _vāta-anulomana_; _apasmāra-hara_ (anticonvulsant, modest); _medhya_ in the calming-clarifying sense; _balya_ (nervous-system-strengthening, in modest dose); _vraṇa-ropaṇa_ (topical wound-healing).

The substance does not appear in Caraka, Suśruta, or the major nighaṇṭus; the placement is reasoned cross-system.

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S, N, F, M)_

- *(F) Conditional / Brazilian folk:* hypotension; pregnancy (universal folk caution); concurrent CNS depressants.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *CNS-depressant additive interaction*: mulungu has *substantial sedative-anxiolytic activity* via GABA-A and nicotinic-cholinergic mechanisms; *concurrent use with alcohol, benzodiazepines, opioids, sedative antihistamines, or other CNS depressants* is contraindicated due to additive sedation. This is the principal clinical-pharmacology caveat.
  - *Antihypertensive interaction*: documented mild antihypertensive activity; additive with antihypertensive drugs.
  - *Surgical / anesthesia interaction*: *the Erythrina alkaloids have neuromuscular-blocking activity* (paralleling the curare-class) at high doses — *discontinue mulungu 7-14 days before surgery* given the theoretical potentiation of neuromuscular-blocking anesthetics. This is documented and clinically relevant.
  - *Pregnancy and lactation*: *contraindicated* — the CNS-active alkaloids cross the placenta and into breast milk.
  - *Pediatric*: avoid; the immature CNS is more susceptible to CNS-depressant effects.
  - *Concurrent psychiatric medication* (SSRIs, MAOIs, tricyclics): theoretical interaction; discuss with prescriber.
  - *Driving and operating machinery*: avoid after mulungu use due to the sedative effect.
  - *Erythrina alkaloid toxicity at very high doses*: documented neuromuscular blockade, respiratory depression, hypotension at substantial overdose. Standard infusion preparations are far from these doses; concentrated extracts and tinctures require careful dose control.
  - *South American Erythrina species as arrow-poison sources*: certain Erythrina species (notably _E. americana_ of Mexico) have documented pre-Columbian use as arrow-poison sources; this register is *not* applicable to _E. mulungu_ at typical Brazilian folk-medical doses but illustrates the genus-level CNS-and-neuromuscular pharmacology.#footnote[CNS-depressant interaction: published modern pharmacological reviews on _Erythrina mulungu_ and _E. velutina_; the GABA-A and nicotinic-cholinergic binding is documented in receptor-binding studies. Surgical anesthesia interaction: theoretical based on Erythrina alkaloid neuromuscular pharmacology; conservative-safety recommendation. South American Erythrina arrow-poison register: standard ethnopharmacological literature.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Not classical in Tibb._ Erythrina mulungu _is exclusively Brazilian and was unknown to the medieval Arabic-Persian medical world; no canonical attestation. No Arabic name. Modern Arabic / Persian / Urdu commercial usage is the transliteration_ mūlūngū / ملونگو. _Reasoned modern Unani placement on the sedative-anxiolytic axis (entirely interpretive):_

*Mizāj* — by extension on the sedative-anxiolytic axis: *Cold and Dry in the 1st-2nd degree* — paralleling the Tibb classification of *gawzaban* (borage, _Borago officinalis_, the canonical Tibb mood-supportive substance) and *saʿtar* (thyme, mildly sedative); the bitter-tannin-cool profile reads as a *muraṭṭib li-l-aʿṣāb* (nerve-moistening / calming) substance with modest cooling action on the heated-nervous register.

*Af'āl* (reasoned): *mufariḥ* (mood-elevating — the modern anxiolytic register); *muqawwī al-rūḥ al-nafsānī* (psychic-spirit tonic — paralleling the broader Tibb classification of CNS-supportive substances); *munawwim* (hypnotic / sleep-inducing — modest); *muḥaffif al-khafqān* (palpitation-reducing — the cardiac-anxiety register); *mubarriḍ li-l-ḥarāra al-nafsāniyya* (cools the heated-psychic state, the Avicennan equivalent of "calms the agitated nerves"); *jālī* (mild cleansing).

*Istemālāt* (reasoned): anxiety; insomnia; cardiac palpitation of nervous-emotional pattern; mood disturbance; restlessness.

*Mawāne'*: hypotension; pregnancy; lactation; concurrent CNS-depressant medication; pediatric use; pre-surgical (the neuromuscular concern).

The substance does not appear in Avicenna, Ibn al-Bayṭār, or Khazāyin al-Adwiya. See _tibb-al-arabi.typ_.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

No classical Chinese formulas use _E. mulungu_. The canonical Chinese 安神 (anshen) sedative-calming class — including 酸棗仁 (suānzǎorén), 遠志 (yuǎn zhì), 茯神 (fú shén), 柏子仁 (bǎi zǐ rén) — covers the analogous indication with different botanicals.

=== Ayurveda

No classical Ayurvedic formulas use _E. mulungu_. The Ayurvedic anxiolytic-sedative register is covered by *brāhmī* (Bacopa monnieri or Centella asiatica — see _centella.typ_), *jaṭāmāṃsī* (Nardostachys jatamansi), *śaṅkhapuṣpī* (Convolvulus pluricaulis), *ashwagandha* (see _ashwagandha.typ_), and other classical _medhya-rasāyana_ drugs.

=== Western / Greco-Roman

No classical European tradition; exclusively American.

=== Tibb (Arabic-Islamic) medicine

No classical Tibb tradition.

=== Brazilian / Indigenous / Afro-Brazilian — the substantive tradition

- *Chá de mulungu (mulungu tea)*: the canonical Brazilian folk preparation — short decoction of ~3-5 g dried bark in 250 mL water, simmered 10-15 minutes; taken in the evening for sleep support and anxiety. *Not for daytime use* due to the sedative effect.
- *Tintura de mulungu* (mulungu tincture): alcoholic maceration of dried bark (1:5 in 40% ethanol); ~1-3 mL diluted in water; concentrated and faster-acting than the tea.
- *Garrafada de mulungu*: Brazilian folk-medical compound decoction in a glass bottle; often combined with *erva-cidreira* (lemon balm or lemongrass), *maracujá* (passion fruit leaf), *camomila* (chamomile), and other Brazilian sedative herbs.
- *Banho de mulungu* (mulungu ritual bath): Afro-Brazilian Candomblé and Umbanda preparation; bark decoction added to bath water for spiritual cleansing and calming; the ritual register associated with *Ossain* (Yoruba-Bantu orixá of medicinal plants) and the broader Afro-Brazilian religious-medical tradition.
- *Modern Brazilian commercial phytotherapeutics*: standardized mulungu extracts in capsule and liquid form, Anvisa-registered; some commercial sleep-aid products combine mulungu with passion fruit extract and valerian.
- *Mulungu + maracujá* (mulungu + passion fruit) — the canonical Brazilian combination for anxiety and insomnia; widely used in Brazilian psychiatric-and-folk register.#footnote[Brazilian folk preparations: standard Brazilian ethnobotanical literature (Lorenzi & Matos; Anvisa monographs); Afro-Brazilian religious-medical literature on banho preparations. Modern phytotherapy products: Anvisa-registered Brazilian commercial phytotherapeutics list.]

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

_E. mulungu_ is principally a *subtropical-humid Brazilian Atlantic Forest and Cerrado* species, with the related _E. velutina_ in the *semi-arid Caatinga* Northeast. See _climates.typ_.

*Subtropical-humid Atlantic Forest (the canonical region for _E. mulungu_).* The climate carries hot wet summers and mild dry winters, with substantial atmospheric humidity year-round; the mulungu tree is large and produces its red-orange flowers in late winter / early spring (August-October in the Southern Hemisphere), with bark harvest typically in the dry season. In Ayurvedic _ṛtucaryā_ terms the climate carries _grīṣma_-_varṣā_ Pitta-Kapha load with mild winter Vāta; in 中醫 terms the climate is 暑濕 (summer-heat-damp) on the Spleen-Heart; in Tibb terms the constitutional load is Hot-Moist (balgham) in summer. *The traditional response and the mulungu register*: mulungu's cooling-bitter-sedative character is constitutionally apt for the *summer-Pitta and chronic-heat-induced* anxiety-and-insomnia patterns typical of the Brazilian Atlantic Forest climate, where the heat-and-humidity combination predictably generates the anxious-agitated mental register; the *bark-as-cooling-sedative* preparation addresses this constitutionally. The pairing with *maracujá* (also cooling-sedative) intensifies the constitutional alignment.

*Semi-arid Caatinga (the canonical region for _E. velutina_ — suinã).* The Caatinga climate carries hot dry summers, brief intense rainy seasons, and extreme thermal range; the *E. velutina* (suinã) tree is adapted to the drought-and-heat stress and produces particularly robust bark. The Caatinga's *heat-and-dryness* constitutional load is Pitta-Vāta dominant; suinã's cooling-bitter character is constitutionally apt and is the canonical Northeastern-Brazilian folk anxiolytic.

*Afro-Brazilian climate-cultural overlay.* The substance's African-Bantu cultural heritage reflects the *parallel African-tropical climate* of the source Bantu cultures (Angola, Congo, Lower-Guinea coast), where related Erythrina species have parallel medical-and-ritual use; the *cross-Atlantic cultural-and-medical transmission* preserved both the substance category and the constitutional-pharmacological register essentially intact.

*Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26).* Mulungu exported globally as Brazilian-folk-medicine anxiolytic reaches every climate. *The anxiolytic-and-sedative indication is essentially climate-neutral* — anxiety and insomnia are universal human conditions across climates, and the GABA-A / nicotinic mechanism is climate-independent. The *constitutional logic* points to evening / nighttime use universally; the substance is one of the cleaner cases of a folk-medicinal anxiolytic whose mechanism-of-action transcends climate. The Brazilian-diaspora communities globally have continued the *chá de mulungu* tradition; the substance is increasingly available in international herbal-supplement commerce as a "natural anxiolytic" alternative.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents:*
- *Erythrina alkaloids* — *the principal active class*; tetracyclic alkaloids characteristic of the genus. Major members in _E. mulungu_ and _E. velutina_: *erysodine*, *erysovine*, *erythraline*, *erythratine*, *erythratidine*, *erythravine*, *hypaphorine* (= tryptophan-betaine, the principal active in some species), *β-erythroidine*. The Erythrina alkaloids bind *nicotinic acetylcholine receptors* (α4β2 and α7 subtypes) as antagonists, and *GABA-A receptors* (positive modulation) — the dual-mechanism CNS pharmacology underlying the anxiolytic-sedative-anticonvulsant register.
- *Flavonoids* — vitexin, isovitexin, schaftoside, abyssinone derivatives, lupinifolin, lupinifolinol.
- *Pterocarpans* — erybraedin A, B, C; the characteristic Fabaceae-pterocarpan class.
- *Triterpenoid saponins* — modest fraction.
- *Tannins* — substantial in bark; condensed tannins.
- *Phenolic acids*.#footnote[Phytochemistry: published Brazilian phytochemistry surveys on _E. mulungu_ and _E. velutina_ in _Journal of Ethnopharmacology_, _Phytochemistry_, _Revista Brasileira de Farmacognosia_; receptor-binding pharmacology studies on Erythrina alkaloids in _Phytomedicine_ and _Pharmacology Biochemistry and Behavior_.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Anxiolytic*: *substantial preclinical and modest clinical evidence* for anxiolytic effect in standard anxiety-behavior models (elevated plus-maze, light-dark transition, social interaction) at doses corresponding to the Brazilian-folk register. Comparison with diazepam in animal models shows comparable anxiolytic activity at higher mulungu doses. Small clinical studies have shown modest anxiolytic effect with reasonable safety in adult human use. The substance is one of the better-characterized Brazilian native medicinals psychopharmacologically.
- *Sedative-hypnotic*: documented in rodent sleep models; sleep-promoting action in modest clinical evidence.
- *Anxiolytic mechanism — GABA-A positive modulation*: erythrina alkaloid binding to the benzodiazepine site of the GABA-A receptor is documented; the mechanism parallels classical benzodiazepines but with substantially milder receptor affinity (hence the gentler clinical profile).
- *Nicotinic acetylcholine receptor antagonism*: erythrina alkaloids are *competitive antagonists* at α4β2 and α7 nAChRs; this mechanism contributes to anxiolysis (the same axis as varenicline, the smoking-cessation drug — though pharmacologically distinct).
- *Anticonvulsant*: preclinical evidence for anticonvulsant activity in pentylenetetrazole and maximal-electroshock models.
- *Antihypertensive*: modest preclinical evidence.
- *Anti-inflammatory*: preclinical broad-spectrum activity, flavonoid- and pterocarpan-mediated.
- *Antimicrobial*: in vitro activity, modest.
- *Neuromuscular-blocking activity at high doses*: documented; the surgical-anesthesia interaction caveat.
- *Cross-system convergence*: the Brazilian-folk anxiolytic-and-sedative-and-sleep-promoting register + reasoned cross-system anxiolytic placement + modern GABA-A and nicotinic-receptor pharmacology converges cleanly. Mulungu is among the better-documented Brazilian native medicinals at the mechanism-of-action level — comparable to passion flower (_Passiflora incarnata_) and German chamomile (_Matricaria chamomilla_) in the broader anxiolytic-herbal class.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Dried bark in decoction*: ~3-5 g per cup, evening; *not for daytime use*; *short-term use* (acute anxiety or insomnia episodes); long-term daily use should be supervised.
- *Tincture (1:5 in 40% ethanol)*: ~1-3 mL evening; or per Anvisa-registered product labeling.
- *Standardized extract*: per commercial product specification.
- *Risk groups (absolute or relative contraindications)*:
  - *Pregnancy and lactation*: *contraindicated*.
  - *Pediatric*: avoid except under qualified clinical supervision.
  - *Concurrent CNS depressants* (alcohol, benzodiazepines, opioids, sedative antihistamines, sedative antidepressants): *contraindicated* (additive sedation).
  - *Concurrent antihypertensive therapy*: monitor.
  - *Pre-surgical* (within 7-14 days of scheduled surgery): *discontinue* (neuromuscular concern).
  - *Severe hypotension*: avoid.
  - *Driving and operating machinery*: avoid after mulungu use.
  - *Concurrent psychiatric medication*: discuss with prescriber.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*

